{"subjectId":"sql","id":"order-by","title":"Sorting with ORDER BY","group":"Querying Basics","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Without <code>ORDER BY</code>, row order is <strong>undefined</strong> — it may look sorted today and change after an index or a version upgrade. If order matters, say so.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-descending\"></i>One or more sort keys</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, dept_id, salary\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY dept_id ASC, salary DESC, name;    -- ASC is the default</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | dept_id | salary\n-------+---------+-------\nAsha   | 1       | 150000\nRavi   | 1       | 120000\nKaran  | 1       | 95000\nMeera  | 1       | 95000\nPriya  | 2       | 110000\nNeha   | 2       | 72000\nArjun  | 2       | 70000\nSara   | 3       | 85000\nVikram | 3       | 80000</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Rows are sorted by the first key; ties are broken by the second, and so on. Meera and Karan tie on salary, so <code>name</code> decides.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Sort by expression, alias or position</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary / 12 AS monthly\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 2\nORDER BY monthly DESC;                      -- alias from the SELECT list\n\nSELECT name, length(name) AS len\nFROM employees\nORDER BY length(name) DESC, 1               -- expression, then column position 1\nLIMIT 3;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | monthly\n------+--------\nPriya | 9166\nNeha  | 6000\nArjun | 5833\n\nname   | len\n-------+----\nVikram | 6\nArjun  | 5\nKaran  | 5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>ORDER BY 1</code> (column position) is handy interactively but fragile in code — reordering the select list changes the sort silently.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i>Where do NULLs go?</h3>\n<p>SQLite, MySQL and SQL Server treat <code>NULL</code> as <strong>smallest</strong> (first in <code>ASC</code>); PostgreSQL and Oracle treat it as <strong>largest</strong> (last in <code>ASC</code>). Be explicit with <code>NULLS FIRST</code>/<code>NULLS LAST</code> (PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQLite 3.30+).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, email FROM employees WHERE emp_id &gt;= 6 ORDER BY email;\nSELECT name, email FROM employees WHERE emp_id &gt;= 6 ORDER BY email NULLS LAST;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | email\n-------+---------------\nNeha   | NULL\nDev    | NULL\nArjun  | arjun@corp.in\nSara   | sara@corp.in\nVikram | vikram@corp.in\n\nname   | email\n-------+---------------\nArjun  | arjun@corp.in\nSara   | sara@corp.in\nVikram | vikram@corp.in\nNeha   | NULL\nDev    | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- engines without NULLS LAST (MySQL, SQL Server): sort on \"is null\" first\nSELECT name, email FROM employees WHERE emp_id &gt;= 6\nORDER BY email IS NULL, email;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | email\n-------+---------------\nArjun  | arjun@corp.in\nSara   | sara@corp.in\nVikram | vikram@corp.in\nNeha   | NULL\nDev    | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Custom order with CASE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT order_id, status FROM orders\nORDER BY CASE status\n             WHEN 'pending'   THEN 1\n             WHEN 'shipped'   THEN 2\n             WHEN 'delivered' THEN 3\n             ELSE 4\n         END,\n         order_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>order_id | status\n---------+----------\n107      | pending\n104      | shipped\n108      | shipped\n101      | delivered\n102      | delivered\n103      | delivered\n106      | delivered\n105      | cancelled</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you ORDER BY a column that is not in the SELECT list?</summary><div><p>Yes, in a plain query (<code>SELECT name FROM employees ORDER BY salary</code>). Not with <code>SELECT DISTINCT</code> or in some <code>UNION</code> cases, because the sort key must then be part of the output rows.</p></div></details></section>"}